/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/ Column expressions & lists: pulling a table's data out of a `select`.
/
/ A *column expression* is `table`col` or a one-column `select`. Outside a
/ table statement it materialises to a *list* (an IntVec / FloatVec / StrVec /
/ SymVec / BoolVec) — reduce it to a scalar, slice it, or index it.
/
/ Run from the repo root:
/   cargo run -- examples/column_expressions.qpl
/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

trades: load "examples/data/trades.parquet"

/ ── a column as a list ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
log "`table`col` and a one-column `select` are equivalent"
prices:  trades`price
prices2: select price from trades
prices

log "a `where` may be attached to a column expression (but not to a persisted list)"
big: trades`price where size > 200
big

/ ── reductions land in a scalar global ──────────────────────────────────────
log "any aggregate verb (max/min/sum/avg/first/last/count/std/...) collapses a column expression to a single value"
top:   max trades`price
mean:  avg select price from trades
first_sym: first trades`sym
top

log "scalars compose with later queries just like any other global"
select sym, price from trades where price >= mean

/ ── slicing with `n#` ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
log "slicing with n# - first three rows, last three rows"
3#trades`price          / first three rows
-3#trades`price          / last three rows
3#select price from trades

/ ── positional indexing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
log "positional indexing: list[i] picks one element, list[i j k] gathers a sub-list"
nums: 10 20 30 40 50
nums[0]                   / an atom: i64: 10
nums[1 3 4]               / a sub-list: i64[3]: 20 40 50
trades`price[2 3]         / the 3rd and 4th prices
(trades`sym)[0]
(trades`price) 2 3        / q-style juxtaposition still works after `(…)`

/ ── bare names resolve at run time ─────────────────────────────────────────
log "a name is looked up as a global, then a lazy frame, then a table"
t2: trades                / copies the table
2#t2                       / `n#` on a whole table stays a table

/ ── vector arithmetic ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/ every list is a Polars Series under the hood, so the usual operators apply
/ elementwise between a list and a scalar, or between two same-length lists
/ (a length-1 side broadcasts).
log "list + scalar, scalar + list, list op list, and comparisons are all elementwise"
nums + 2                  / i64[5]: 12 22 32 42 52
2 * nums                  / i64[5]: 20 40 60 80 100
nums > 25                 / bool[5]: 00111
sizes: trades`size
prices - prices[0]        / every price minus the first row, broadcast

log "a temporal column materialises to its typed list (e.g. TimestampVec), which shifts the same way a temporal scalar does"
ts: trades`ts
ts + 0D00:01:00.000000000 / every timestamp shifted forward one minute

/ ── filtering a list with `where` ───────────────────────────────────────────
/ `<list-expr> where <predicate>` filters a list elementwise; `x` is the
/ current element. This is a different `where` from the one attached to a
/ `` table`col `` column expression (which filters table rows by any other
/ column before projecting) — chaining the two needs parentheses.
log "list `where`: x is the current element, a comma joins predicates with AND"
nums where x > 25              / i64[3]: 30 40 50
nums where x > 10, x < 50      / i64[3]: 20 30 40
prices where x > 400           / filter an already-materialised list by its own values
(prices where x > 200) where x < 415   / chaining needs parens
